.TH ETHER 3
.SH NAME
ether \- the network card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #E /dev
.nf

.B /dev/ether/addr
.B /dev/ether/data
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I ether
device serves the virtio network card as two files under
.BR /dev/ether .
.PP
.B Addr
is the card's hardware address, six bytes, read-only.
.PP
.B Data
carries frames: a read answers one received frame, and a write sends one.
A read with no frame waiting answers zero bytes rather than parking, so a
client polls it the way it polls any file that does not block.
A read is limited to what one reply can carry, under a full ethernet
frame today, which is enough for an address resolution exchange or a
ping and not yet enough for a full-sized segment.
.PP
.B #E
comes up only over a card the virtio driver found, and is bound at
.B /dev
as a second member, after
.BR #c .
A machine with no card gets no
.B #E
and no
.BR /dev/ether ,
the way one with no disk gets no
.BR /dev/sd0 .
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/ether/ether.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR cons (3),
.IR sd (3),
.IR net (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Writing a frame the card refuses to send fails with
.BR EIO .
Writing to
.B addr
fails with
.BR EPERM .
